<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Copenhagen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Copenhagen is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, on the island of Zealand facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">Øresund</a>. A fishing and trading settlement fortified by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absalon">Absalon</a> in the 1160s, it became the royal residence and capital in the 15th century and grew rich on Baltic trade and the Sound Toll. It was the seat of government of Denmark–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> until 1814.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/copenhagen.jpg" alt="Copenhagen" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Moahim · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2018_-_Christiansborg_from_the_Marble_Bridge.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Copenhagen lies on the eastern shore of the island of Zealand, partly on the island of Amager and on a number of natural and artificial islets between the two. It faces the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">Øresund</a>, the strait that separates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and connects the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>. The Swedish city of Malmö lies directly across the sound, 42 kilometres away by road.</p><p>The Danish name København reflects the city's origin as a harbour and a place of commerce. The Old Norse form was Kaupmannahǫfn, meaning 'merchants' harbour', and the present form developed through sound changes that took place around 1400. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> name Hafnia is the source of the name of the element hafnium.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Flint tools show settlement in the Stone Age, and many historians believe the town dates to the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a>, possibly founded by Sweyn I Forkbeard. The first habitations were probably centred on Gammel Strand, 'old shore'. The natural harbour and good herring stocks attracted seasonal fishermen and merchants from the 11th century and more permanent residents in the 13th. Excavations have uncovered the remains of a large merchant mansion from about 1020 near Kongens Nytorv, and a well from the late 12th century in Pilestræde.</p><p>Copenhagen became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in the early 15th century, and the University of Copenhagen was founded in 1479. In the 16th century it served as the de facto capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-union">Kalmar Union</a> and its monarchy, and by the 17th century it was the centre of the Danish government and military. The 18th century brought a devastating plague outbreak and urban fires, followed by the building of the district of Frederiksstaden and of institutions such as the Royal Theatre and the Royal Academy of Fine Arts. In this period the city was the centre of the Danish slave trade.</p><p>A British fleet bombarded the city in 1807 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>. The Danish Golden Age that followed gave Copenhagen a neoclassical look. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the Finger Plan guided the growth of housing and businesses along five urban railway routes running out from the centre.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Copenhagen is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and its cultural, economic and governmental centre. Since the completion of the Øresund Bridge it has been increasingly integrated with Malmö and the Swedish province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a> in the Øresund Region. Its landmarks include Tivoli Gardens, the Amalienborg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osu-castle">Christiansborg</a> palaces, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosenborg-castle">Rosenborg Castle</a> and Frederik's Church, and the University of Copenhagen is the oldest university in Denmark.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Copenhagen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Copenhagen</b>, the capital, grew into the largest city in Scandinavia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1367 the towns formed the Confederation of Cologne with Holstein, Mecklenburg and Swedish allies, ravaged the Danish coasts and took <b>Copenhagen</b>, and the Peace of Stralsund of 1370 gave them control of the Scanian fortresses for fifteen years and a voice in the Danish succession.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Towns such as Ribe, Roskilde, Lund and later <b>Copenhagen</b> were small by continental standards, and the Hanseatic League dominated much of their trade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In union with Denmark, Norway was ruled from <b>Copenhagen</b>, often through a viceroy (stattholder), and after 1660 under absolute monarchy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Icelandic scholar Árni Magnússon, who lived from 1663 to 1730, collected most of the surviving manuscripts in <b>Copenhagen</b>, saving many from loss, though the fire of Copenhagen in 1728 destroyed part of his library.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-norse">Old Norse · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li><li><blockquote>The Danish historian Mogens Herman Hansen, who led the <b>Copenhagen</b> Polis Centre’s comparison of some thirty city-state cultures, defined it as “a self-governing, but not necessarily independent, political unit”: many city-states acknowledged an overlord or belonged to a league while running their own affairs.</blockquote><a href="/government/city-state">City-state · How It Works</a></li></ul></section>
